\newlabel{fig:speed_moderation}{{4}{33}{Response Time Moderation Across User Experience. Bars show the interaction coefficient between the treatment effect and log response time, from separate Cox models by tenure bucket. Positive values indicate that longer response times are associated with a stronger reciprocity effect. Error bars indicate 95\% confidence intervals.\relax }{figure.caption.23}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {5.6}The Session-Exit Mechanism}{33}{subsection.5.6}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces Help rate during the post-question window, separately for users who have not yet received an answer (``No answer yet,'' blue) and users who have already received one (``Answer received,'' red), by tenure bucket. The green dashed line shows the cumulative share of users who have received an answer by each hour. For experienced users (all panels except $<$\,1 Week), the help rate of users still waiting (blue) consistently exceeds the help rate of users who have already received an answer (red) up to approximately 1--2 hours post-question. This pattern indicates that receiving an answer is associated with platform \textit  {exit} rather than continued engagement: users who get a quick answer resolve their need and leave, while users still waiting remain on the platform and are more likely to help others.\relax }}{35}{figure.caption.24}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:help_rate_adoption}{{5}{35}{Help rate during the post-question window, separately for users who have not yet received an answer (``No answer yet,'' blue) and users who have already received one (``Answer received,'' red), by tenure bucket. The green dashed line shows the cumulative share of users who have received an answer by each hour. For experienced users (all panels except $<$\,1 Week), the help rate of users still waiting (blue) consistently exceeds the help rate of users who have already received an answer (red) up to approximately 1--2 hours post-question. This pattern indicates that receiving an answer is associated with platform \textit {exit} rather than continued engagement: users who get a quick answer resolve their need and leave, while users still waiting remain on the platform and are more likely to help others.\relax }{figure.caption.24}{}}
